    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking at some data for a new low-fat mayo formulation and hitting some stability issues in our accelerated aging tests. The pH is quite low (around 3.5) and we're seeing significant phase separation after 48 hours. Can you walk me through the specific role lecithin plays in stabilizing the oil-water interface in these high-acid environments?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Under the acidic conditions of your low-fat mayo, lecithin acts as a molecular bridge, but in this high-acid environment (pH 3.5), it's more like a goblin trying to hold back a tidal wave of phase separation. Lecithin is a phospholipid with a hydrophilic head and hydrophobic tail, which is perfect for the oil-water interface, but at low pH, the phospholipid heads can become protonated, altering their charge and reducing their ability to form a stable barrier. This is why you're seeing separation after just 48 hours; the emulsion is breaking down because the stabilizer isn't holding the oil droplets in place as effectively as it would at a neutral pH.\n\nTo fix this, you might need to consider adding a co-stabilizer, like a protein or a specific type of gum, to reinforce the interface, or perhaps adjust the lecithin source to one that remains stable at lower pH levels. It's a classic case of trying to keep a goblin from eating your lunch; you need a stronger barrier or a different strategy to keep the ingredients together. In the world of food science, stability is everything, and without it",
      "source": "target",
